|>	/genvmunix

The generic kernel that was running during the installation process.  This supports all processors and just about all devices.  It's bigger and slower than
a kernel you will build but you might consider leaving it as a safety net - a 
kernel you know will boot in case /vmunix gets trashed.  Of course, you could 
create your own safety net and delete genvmunix.

|>	
|>	/vmunix
|>
|>	/usr/users/vmunix

Probably created by a user on your system.

|>
|>	/usr/sys/NAME/vmunix.NAME
|>
|>	/usr/sys/NAME/vmunix.sys

The two above are linked and are the product of the kernel build.  Once you copy
this kernel to /vmunix, you don't have to save these.

|>
|>	/usr/sys/NAME/vmunix.swap

Not sure exactly, but it is small.  Probably unneeded, but like I said, I'm unsure.

