Attached is a summary of the problem I posted couple of days
ago regarding the set up of Gradient's PC-DCE run-time product.

The remainder of this posting is contains the solution to our
problem, the original problem description and some additional 
information from Gradient.

THE SOLUTION:

We had to put the hostname and IP address for the DCE services in 
the local host table for the FTP Software TCP/IP transport that 
we were using on the PC.  We needed this even though the hostname
and IP address were in the DNS. 

THE PROBLEM DESCRIPTON:
    
>We've recently gotten PC-DCE from Gradient Technologies and are 
>experiencing problems in getting a MS/Windows client to 
>login into the DCE cell.  The symptoms are as follows:
>
>o  After configuring the runtime support for PC-DCE and rebooting
>   the PC and restarting Windows, we get the following error message 
>   when we attempt to do a dce login:
>
>    User Identification Failure - KDC can't fulfill requested 
>    option (dce/krb) 
>
>o  If we now attempt another dce login again we get the following 
>   error message:
>
>    User Identification Failure - status 00000002 (unknown facility)
>
>o  If we attempt to use the same principal id and dce login from a
>   UNIX box (Solaris 2.3), the login completes successfully.  So the
>   principal id is valid.
>
>Finally,  as alluded to in the last bullet item, the DCE cell is
>composed of Sun systems with the DCE support from Transarc.

ADDITIONAL INFORMATION FROM GRADIENT:

                PC-DCE supports both WinSock and the proprietary
        sockets implementation from FTP for their PCTCP.  There's
        a problem with the proprietary support in that PC-DCE requires
        the local hosts table for name resolution.  This is not the
        case with PC-DCE's (preferred) WinSock support, so the
        *official* recommendation is that you re-install PC-DCE, and
        this time choose the WinSock selection from the "available
        transports" list.  One other note, since you didn't mention
        what version of PCTCP you're using, if it's prior to 2.3
        than you'll need to get the latest "winsock.dll" from FTP
        via anonymous FTP.  (ftp.ftp.com   in /support/winsock/winsock.exe).
        This is a self exploding .exe that will produce a "winsock.dll.


