In article <1994Jun15.214753.10001@wavehh.hanse.de> cracauer@wavehh.hanse.de (Martin Cracauer) writes:
>> <description of 110 Kbyte file taking up 8 Kbyte more than expected deleted>
>
>OSF/1 uses a BSD Filesystem, does it? Then there are fragments where
>the ends of up to 4 different files can be stored into one block.
>-- 

The thing they are complaining about is the presence of an indirect block for
any file larger than 13*8 Kbyte (the first 13 blocks are direct blocks). 
Changing the block size to 4 Kbyte would spare them half of that indirect
block (but cannot be done, because it would ne almost impossible to have
a smaller file system block size than page size).

The correct solution for them is to use Advfs instead of UFS, as it can
represent such files without indirects (it uses extents).

